From 3c869f1df1f0bd2b537aa221303bbc465d340e9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:33:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] leds: rgb: ktd202x: Give each blinking LED a timer of
 its own

Every LED blinks with timer 1, so when a second LED blinks, its on time
and period silently replace those of the first one, while the sysfs of
the first one still shows its old delays. The same happens between a
blink and a hardware pattern.

The chip has a second timer with its own on time, Reg3, and only the
flash period and the ramp times are shared. Give each blinking LED one
of the two timers, and keep the shared registers as long as a LED uses
them: a LED that asks for another period or other ramps gets -EBUSY,
which makes the LED core blink it in software instead. The shared
registers are only written when they change, so that the other timer is
not restarted.

Track the timer in the LED instead of reading the channel state back,
and also never program an on time of zero for a hardware pattern.

Signed-off-by: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
 Documentation/leds/leds-ktd202x.rst |   8 +-
 drivers/leds/rgb/leds-ktd202x.c     | 236 ++++++++++++++++++----------
